-- Chapter 5: A short history of Assamese literature
Jnanpith Award, Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jnanpith_Award$t$, date '2026-11-24' from public.content where slug = $t$assamese-literature$t$ on conflict do nothing;
Madhav Kandali (14th century): wrote the Saptakanda Ramayana, one of the earliest versions of the Ramayana in an Indo-Aryan language.
Srimanta Sankardev: his greatest work is the Kirtan Ghosha; he also created {{term:borgeet|Borgeet}} and {{term:ankiya-naat|Ankiya Naat}}.
Madhavdev: his greatest work is the Naam Ghosha, which has one thousand verses.$t$, null from public.content where slug = $t$assamese-literature$t$ on conflict do nothing;
1889: the magazine Jonaki begins the {{term:jonaki-era|Jonaki era}}; Lakshminath Bezbaroa introduces the short story.
1894: Mirijiyori by Rajanikanta Bordoloi, the first Assamese novel, is published.
1917: the Asam Sahitya Sabha is formed.$t$, null from public.content where slug = $t$assamese-literature$t$ on conflict do nothing;
